# Create a file with spaces
touch "my important file.txt"

# WRONG - tries to delete "my", "important", and "file.txt"
file="my important file.txt"
rm $file
# BREAKS!

# CORRECT - deletes the single file
rm "$file"

-----------------------

Example:
hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ touch "my important file.txt"

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ ls
'my important file.txt'   notes

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ file="my important file.txt"

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ echo ${file}
my important file.txt

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ rm $file
rm: cannot remove 'my': No such file or directory
rm: cannot remove 'important': No such file or directory
rm: cannot remove 'file.txt': No such file or directory

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
❯ rm "$file"

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main [?]
➜ ls
notes


-----

Command Substitutions

Command outputs can be captured into variables.


Example:

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ today=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ echo "today is $today"
today is 2026-09-16

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ echo "today is "$today""
today is 2026-09-16

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ current_dir=$(pwd)

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ branch=$(git branch --show-current)

hunter in 🌐 ubuntu in lab/Bash on  main
➜ echo "Current directory is ${current_dir}" && echo "Current branch is ${branch}"

Current directory is /home/hunter/Projects/lab/Bash
Current branch is main
